/*
 * What this compiler decided about a type - the rows of `TypeTraits(a)`, and the whole content of a
 * `Node::Metric`.
 *
 * `YANA_MIR_METRIC( enumerator, name )`. The name is what the IR prints, so a reader of a dump and a
 * reader of this file see the same word; there is nothing else on a row, because a metric *is* a
 * question and the answering is `resolve_ssa`'s.
 *
 * The list is `TypeTraits`' field list in `lib/Native/Pointer.yana`, in the same order, and the
 * agreement is checked rather than assumed - `expandTypeTraits` compares the record's field count
 * against this table's length and reports a library that has drifted. That check is the reason the
 * order matters: a sixth field added in source with no row here would otherwise be built out of
 * whatever the fifth left behind.
 *
 * **Two shapes of answer, and every row has both.** At a type this compilation laid out, a metric is
 * an immediate. At an erased type parameter it is a read of the descriptor the caller supplied,
 * which is why none of these folds in MIR: a body compiled once for every `a` has no layout to fold
 * against, and a stage that answered here would be answering a question it cannot see.
 *
 * What is deliberately *not* here: `Count`, which the shipping stage carries as a metric. A const
 * parameter is `Node::GenValue` in this IR - a number has nothing to describe, so it is a slot read
 * and one step shorter rather than one longer - and it would not share this node's one operand-free
 * shape honestly.
 *
 * A **byte order** was the other, and it is now not a metric in either stage: it is a fact about the
 * machine rather than about any `of`, so it is `supplied.def`'s `byteOrder` - a global of the
 * library's own `Endian` whose constant the compiler writes, exactly as `wordBits` and
 * `vectorBytes` are.
 */

// What one value occupies, and what a whole-value copy moves. Not rounded up to the alignment.
YANA_MIR_METRIC( Size,        "size"_v         )
YANA_MIR_METRIC( Align,       "align"_v        )

/*
 * What indexing homogeneous storage advances by: the size rounded up to the alignment.
 *
 * The one measurement raw-pointer code gets wrong, and the reason this table exists before the other
 * four had a caller: a record's trailing padding belongs to whoever contains it, so `data P {a: I64,
 * b: U8}` is nine bytes and sixteen apart, and arithmetic in sizes overlaps its own elements.
 *
 * Derived from the two above rather than stored beside them - see `kDescriptorMetrics`, where the
 * cost of the derivation and the day it stops being one are both written down.
 */
YANA_MIR_METRIC( Stride,      "stride"_v       )

/*
 * Two ownership facts, as `Bool`.
 *
 * **Facts about a representation, never permissions.** A body compiled against an unconstrained `a`
 * is checked as though both answers were the pessimistic one whatever they come out as at one call
 * site, so a program may read them to pick between two paths that do the same thing - a block copy
 * against an element loop - and may not read them to decide what it owns. Only a declared `Copy` or
 * `Drop` constraint changes what a body may do.
 *
 * These are the two rows that need a bit rather than a number, and the two bits come out of the
 * *alignment* where the erased descriptor packs all five - see `kAlignShift`, where why they come
 * from there rather than from the size is argued.
 */
YANA_MIR_METRIC( TrivialCopy, "trivial.copy"_v )
YANA_MIR_METRIC( HasTeardown, "has.teardown"_v )
